          "chunk_id": "chunk:ch17_biotechnology_and_genomics:22",
          "text": "or the host DNA.\n\nPlasmids occur naturally in bacterial populations (such as Escherichia coli) and have genes that can contribute\nfavorable traits to the organism, such as antibiotic resistance (the ability to be unaffected by antibiotics). Scientists\nhave repurposed and engineered plasmids as vectors for molecular cloning and the large-scale production of\nimportant reagents, such as insulin and human growth hormone. An important feature of plasmid vectors is the ease\nwith which scientists can introduce a foreign DNA fragment via the multiple cloning site (MCS). The MCS is a short\nDNA sequence containing multiple sites that different commonly available restriction endonucleases can cut.",
          "source_name": "data/Ch17_Biotechnology_and_Genomics.pdf",
          "chunk_index": 22,
